.TH CAMSRV 4
.SH NAME
camsrv \- a camera as a live video feed
.SH SYNOPSIS
.B mount {camsrv} /mnt/video
.br
.B mount {camsrv -t} /mnt/video
.br
.B mount {camsrv -l
.I file
.B } /mnt/video
.br
.B mount {camsrv -r
.I degrees
.B } /mnt/video
.SH DESCRIPTION
.I Camsrv
serves a Raspberry Pi Camera Module 3 (Sony IMX708) or Camera Module 2
(IMX219) in the schema of
.IR vid9p ,
so what shows a video feed shows a camera, here or across a mount:
.BR wm/camera ,
.BR vidplay9p ,
and Matrix's
.BR video-pane .
With
.B -t
it serves a simulated sensor, on any machine: colour bars and a moving
band, as raw samples, through the same code.
.PP
Which camera is attached is found when
.I camsrv
starts: it powers the sensor long enough to ask the Camera Module 3's
address and then the Camera Module 2's, and
.B status
says which
.RB ( sensor=imx708
or
.BR sensor=imx219 ).
Each is read out two by two binned, RAW10 on two lanes: 2304\(mu1296 on
the Camera Module 3, 1640\(mu1232 on the Camera Module 2, and
.B mode
takes those divided by an even number. The Camera Module 2's lens is
fixed: it refuses
.BR focus ,
and its
.B gain
codes are 0 to 232 (the analogue gain 256/(256\-code)).
.PP
To run the camera in one Dis VM
.RI ( vm (1))
and read it from any other, post its mount
.RI ( post (1)):
.IP
.EX
vm 1 sh -c 'mount -A {camsrv} /mnt/video; post cam /mnt/video'
mount -A '#scam/fs' /mnt/video
.EE
.PP
Every connection sees the one camera: frame
.I N
is the same frame on all of them, and the sensor runs while any of them
reads it.
.PP
With
.B -l
it appends its
.B status
line to
.IR file ,
seconds since boot first, once a minute, which is how a soak reads it
from outside the namespace the feed is mounted in.
.PP
With
.BR -r ,
the picture is turned clockwise by
.IR degrees ,
0, 90, 180 or 270, for a camera mounted on its side or upside down;
the same as
.B rotate
below, from the start. How the camera is mounted is a fact about the
installation, so it is whoever starts
.I camsrv
that says so, not the readers: each of them gets the picture the right
way up, sized as turned. On a bare-metal Pi 4 the card's
.B camera
file is where that is said: the system starts
.I camsrv
on
.B /mnt/video
at boot and writes the file's lines to
.BR ctl .
.PP
.I Camsrv
is the only thing that knows the sensor. It drives it over
.IR i2c (3),
takes raw frames from
.IR camera (3),
and turns them into pictures with
.BR $Bayer .
The sensor is on only while something has
.B frame
or
.B raw
open, and the last close powers it down.
.TP
.B 0/fmt
.RI `` w
.I h
.B i420
.IR fps ''.
.TP
.B 0/frame
I420, full range, numbered as
.IR vid9p 's
frames are: frame
.I N
is the frame size's bytes at offset
.IR N ×framesize,
the same for every reader, so a record keyed
.BI frame= N
.RI ( layers (2))
means the same frame to all of them.
The last eight frames are kept; a read below them is refused with
.LR "frame expired" ,
and a read past the newest waits for that frame; one more than eight
frames past it is refused as one below is.
A live reader starts at the newest
.RB ( status 's
.B frames=
less one, if that is not below
.BR first= )
and goes on from the newest again when it falls behind.
Numbers run on across the sensor going off and on.
Each frame has the width and height it was made at.
A reader asking for one of another size than the reader last had
(its
.B fmt
when it opened
.BR frame ,
or the last change it was told of) is refused once with
.LR "format changed: \fIw h\fP i420 \fIfps\fP" ,
that frame's format; it takes it and reads the frame again.
This is what
.B rotate
while streaming gives: a quarter turn keeps the frame size, so frame
.I N
stays where it was.
.TP
.B 0/raw
A frame as the sensor gave it, 16 bits a sample, little-endian, at the
size and mosaic in
.BR status .
One frame an open: the newest, copied when it was made, so the next
frame does not overwrite it while it is read; offsets past it are the
end of the file.
.TP
.B 0/status
One line of
.IB key = value\f1:
.B frames=
(one past the newest frame) and
.B first=
(the oldest kept), and no
.BR pos= :
this is what marks a feed live to its readers.
.B stream=
is the capture's state:
.B on
while the sensor runs,
.B stopping
between the last close and the capture's end (the frame it was making
is finished first), and
.B off
otherwise; with readers and
.BR error= ,
the sensor would not run.
.BR autofocus=
says how the last run went.
.B rawformat=
is the mosaic as the sensor reads it out now
.RB ( rggb ,
.BR grbg ,
.B gbrg
or
.BR bggr :
.B flip
changes it), the sample width and the raw size.
.TP
.B 0/layer/
Empty as served: where what is drawn over the frames is bound, a file a
layer, stacked in the union's order
.RI ( layers (2)).
.TP
.B 0/ctl
Writes, one at a time; reading gives the settings.
It is written by the user
.I camsrv
runs as, who owns the files, and read by anyone.
A write that is not one of these, or whose value is missing, is no
number or is out of range, is refused and changes nothing.
The settings marked ``not while streaming'' are refused while
.B frame
or
.B raw
is open; written just after the last close, they wait for the capture
to have stopped.
.RS
.PD 0
.TP
.BI mode " w h
Picture size: 576 324 or 1152 648, or 324 576 or 648 1152 turned a
quarter. Not while streaming.
.TP
.BI fps " n
1 to the sensor's most, from its timing: 56 for the Camera Module 3,
41 for the Camera Module 2. Not while streaming.
.TP
.BI exposure " lines
0, the default, is automatic.
.TP
.BI gain " code
Analogue gain, 0 to 960, of 1024\-1024/\fIgain\fR.
.TP
.BI focus " n
The lens, 0 to 1023. Taken at any time, and applied when the sensor
starts.
.TP
.B focus auto
Contrast autofocus, once: a few seconds, needing a still picture with
detail in it. A picture that moves, or has nothing to focus on, leaves
the lens where it was, and
.B status
says which.
.TP
.BI flip " n
1 mirrors, 2 turns upside down. 3, the default, is right way up as the
module usually sits. This is the sensor's own register: it changes the
raw frames too. Taken while streaming: the sensor reads out the new
mirror from its next frame, and the colours are merged in its order from
then.
.TP
.BI rotate " degrees
Turn the picture clockwise: 0, 90, 180 or 270. A quarter turn swaps
.B fmt 's
width and height.
.B raw
is not turned. Taken while streaming: frames made after it are turned,
and readers are told
.L "format changed"
at the first of them.
.PD
.RE
.SH EXAMPLES
A still picture is one frame, cut from the stream with
.IR read (1),
whose
.I count
is the frame size,
.IR w × h ×3/2
for
.BR fmt 's
size: 189420 at 410\(mu308.
.IP
.EX
mount -A {camsrv} /mnt/video
read 189420 < /mnt/video/0/frame > still.i420
.EE
.PP
The same works for any feed in this schema,
.IR vid9p 's
recordings and a camera mounted from another machine alike: the feed
serves frames and knows nothing of picture formats; converting one is
a filter's job.
.PP
.I Cp
will not do: a feed's
.B frame
is a stream that does not end, and
.I cp
copies until it does.
.PP
A video with known answers to point the camera at during a soak:
exposure steps, white balance, a focus chart, motion:
.IP
.B tools/camera-test-pattern.py loop.mp4
.PP
Its segments, their times and the code on every frame that names its
segment and frame are in
.BR docs/CAMERA-TEST-VIDEO.md .
.PP
A capture told to stop is given three seconds: one in a frame that
never comes (the link to the sensor dead) is then killed and the sensor
put off, its readers told
.LR "the sensor stopped answering" .
The simulated sensor
.RB ( -t )
takes one more
.B ctl
write,
.BR stall ,
after which its next frame never comes, for tests of that.
.PP
.I Camsrv
runs in a process group of its own. To stop it, unmount it; to kill
it, write
.B killgrp
to the
.B ctl
file of its process: killing one of its processes alone leaves the
mount without a server and its readers waiting.
.SH SOURCE
.B /appl/cmd/camsrv.b
.br
.B /libinterp/bayer.c
.SH SEE ALSO
.IR camera (3),
.IR i2c (3),
.IR layers (2),
.IR post (1),
.IR videnc (1),
.IR wm-camera (1)
.SH BUGS
There is no ISP. White balance assumes the picture averages to grey, so
a screenful of one colour comes out washed out, and there is no lens
shading correction, so the corners are dark.
